win32u: paint a newly shown window's client area as part of the show

Hog PC's monitor-win32-golden.exe creates its "Processor" window (Qt5151QWindow, WS_POPUP,
0x96000000) and shows it with ShowWindow(SW_SHOWNORMAL); under Wine the window keeps a
non-empty update region (= the whole 770x370 client) and the application pumps
PeekMessage(NULL,0,0,PM_REMOVE) continuously, but it is never handed a WM_PAINT: BeginPaint /
EndPaint / GetUpdateRect / InvalidateRect / UpdateWindow / RedrawWindow are all 0 in a 30 s
IAT capture, and the window stays at the class background.  Windows runs the same exe/argv and
gets BeginPaint + EndPaint, so its client area is painted once, as expected for a window that
has just been shown.

set_window_pos() already has a "Give newly shown windows a chance to redraw" block for exactly
this transition, but it only calls erase_now(): that sends WM_NCPAINT / WM_ERASEBKGND (both are
seen in the Wine window-proc capture) and leaves the client paint to the queued WM_PAINT that
the server synthesises in get_message().  That synthesis only happens when no posted message
(and no input) is pending - WM_PAINT has the lowest priority - so a newly shown window can stay
unpainted forever when the application's message queue never drains.  That is what happens
here: in a +relay capture of the monitor the outer PeekMessage(NULL,0,0,PM_REMOVE) returned a
message 3407 times and "no message" zero times, so the server's WM_PAINT branch was never
reached, even though the window's update region was pending the whole time (GetUpdateRgn keeps
returning SIMPLEREGION box=(0,0)-(770,370)).  Windows delivers that first paint as part of
showing the window, before the application's own message traffic can starve it.

Fix it where the visibility transition is handled: when a window is newly shown, invalidate its
whole client area and update it right away, instead of only erasing it.  The invalidation also
covers the case where the server's expose pass did not leave a pending client region, and it
matches the observable Windows behaviour (a newly shown window is fully invalid and gets its
first WM_PAINT; subsequent paints still go through the normal queued path).

Only the window being shown is touched (no RDW_ALLCHILDREN), and only on a real hidden ->
visible transition: fixup_swp_flags() clears SWP_SHOWWINDOW when the window is already visible,
so the condition is the same one that already guarded erase_now().

--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -4089,6 +4089,7 @@
     struct window_rects old_rects, new_rects;
     UINT orig_flags, context;
     BOOL ret = FALSE;
+    BOOL need_update = FALSE;
 
     orig_flags = winpos->flags;
 
@@ -4197,6 +4198,7 @@
                 && !(orig_flags & SWP_AGG_NOCLIENTCHANGE) && (orig_flags & SWP_SHOWWINDOW))
         {
             erase_now(winpos->hwnd, 0);
+            need_update = TRUE;
         }
     }
 
@@ -4220,6 +4222,16 @@
     if ((winpos->flags & (SWP_NOSIZE|SWP_NOMOVE|SWP_FRAMECHANGED)) != (SWP_NOSIZE|SWP_NOMOVE))
         NtUserNotifyWinEvent( EVENT_OBJECT_LOCATIONCHANGE, winpos->hwnd, OBJID_WINDOW, 0 );
 
+    if (need_update)
+    {
+        /* A window that is being shown has no valid contents yet, and Windows paints it as part
+         * of the show.  Invalidate the whole client area and paint it now; the queued WM_PAINT
+         * alone is not reliable, since it is only generated when the message queue is otherwise
+         * empty and an application with a busy event loop (e.g. Qt reposting wakeup messages)
+         * may never let it be delivered, leaving the window permanently unpainted. */
+        NtUserRedrawWindow( winpos->hwnd, NULL, NULL, RDW_INVALIDATE | RDW_ERASE | RDW_UPDATENOW );
+    }
+
     ret = TRUE;
 done:
     set_thread_dpi_awareness_context( context );
